Build Specification — App 11: Effective-Null Builder
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
A library or command-line tool that constructs the effective null after selection and measurement. It applies a base null, a selection function, and a measurement kernel in the specified order, renormalises, returns the effective null, and warns when it has atoms. It takes in a base null, a selection function, a measurement kernel, and the order, and puts out the effective null and its continuity status. It is for anyone whose pipeline includes selection or measurement error.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept a base null density.

Accept a selection function.

Accept a measurement kernel.

Accept the order of selection and measurement.

Apply the base null, the selection function, and the measurement kernel in the specified order.

Renormalise the effective null over the support.

Return the effective null as a callable density and as a cumulative distribution function.

Warn when the effective null has atoms.

Warn when the effective null has discontinuities.

Warn when the effective null is not continuous, which removes the distribution-free shortcut of Section 3 of the Null-Model Protocol paper.

Support the following base null types: uniform, parametric (normal, exponential, log-normal, power law, gamma, beta), mixture, empirical, and kernel density estimate (KDE).

Support the following selection function types: analytic (closed-form), tabulated (interpolated), and stepwise.

Support the following measurement kernel types: Gaussian (homoscedastic), Gaussian (heteroscedastic), uniform, exponential, and user-supplied.

Support the order "selection on true values, then measurement" (the observed density is proportional to (p_0 * s) convolved with K).

Support the order "selection on measured values, then measurement" (the observed density is proportional to (p_0 convolved with K) * s).

Compute the effective null's probability mass over any window w.

Report the effective null's continuity status: continuous, continuous_with_discontinuities, atomic, or discrete.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same inputs produce same outputs.

No network dependency.

Fast: compute an effective null in under one second for typical inputs.

Versioned: the effective null records the schema version and the builder version.

Reproducible: the effective null records all inputs and the computation order.

Input Specification
Input 1. Base null density. Required. One of: uniform, parametric, mixture, empirical, KDE.

Input 2. Selection function. Optional. Required if selection effects are present.

Input 3. Measurement kernel. Optional. Required if measurement uncertainty is present.

Input 4. Order of selection and measurement. Required if both selection and measurement are present. Values: selection_on_true_then_measurement, selection_on_measured.

Input 5. Support. Required. The support [a, b] over which the effective null is defined.

Input 6. Renormalisation rule. Required. Values: renormalise_over_support (default), truncate.

Input 7. Continuity check tolerance. Optional. The tolerance for detecting atoms and discontinuities. Default 1e-6.

Input 8. Output grid. Optional. The grid of points at which to evaluate the effective null. Default: 1000 points over the support.

Input 9. Schema version. Required. Selects the correct field interpretation.

Output Specification
Output 1. Effective null density. A callable density function, plus an array of values on the output grid.

Output 2. Effective null cumulative distribution function. A callable CDF, plus an array of values on the output grid.

Output 3. Continuity status. Text. One of: continuous, continuous_with_discontinuities, atomic, discrete.

Output 4. Atom list. Array of atom objects. Each atom has a location and a mass.

Output 5. Discontinuity list. Array of discontinuity objects. Each has a location and a jump size.

Output 6. Probability mass over a window. A callable that computes the integral of the effective null over any window w.

Output 7. Warnings. Array of warning objects. Each warning has a code, a message, and the offending field.

Output 8. Reproducibility record. JSON. The schema version, builder version, all inputs, and the computation order.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Base Null Loader
Reads the base null density. Supports uniform, parametric, mixture, empirical, and KDE.

For uniform: reads the support [a, b]. The density is 1 / (b - a) on [a, b] and 0 elsewhere.

For parametric: reads the family and the parameters. Supported families: normal, exponential, log-normal, power law, gamma, beta.

For mixture: reads the components and their weights. Each component is a base null.

For empirical: reads a sample. The density is a step function over the empirical distribution, or a smoothed version.

For KDE: reads a sample and a bandwidth rule. The density is the kernel density estimate.

Module 2: Selection Function Loader
Reads the selection function. Supports analytic, tabulated, and stepwise.

For analytic: reads a closed-form expression or a named function with parameters.

For tabulated: reads a table of (x, s(x)) pairs. The function is interpolated.

For stepwise: reads a set of intervals with constant s(x) in each interval.

Module 3: Measurement Kernel Loader
Reads the measurement kernel. Supports Gaussian (homoscedastic), Gaussian (heteroscedastic), uniform, exponential, and user-supplied.

For Gaussian (homoscedastic): reads the standard deviation sigma.

For Gaussian (heteroscedastic): reads a function sigma(x) or a table of (x, sigma) pairs.

For uniform: reads the half-width delta.

For exponential: reads the rate lambda.

For user-supplied: reads a callable kernel K(x, y).

Module 4: Order Resolver
Reads the order of selection and measurement.

For selection_on_true_then_measurement: the effective null is proportional to (p_0 * s) convolved with K.

For selection_on_measured: the effective null is proportional to (p_0 convolved with K) * s.

Module 5: Effective Null Constructor
Applies the base null, the selection function, and the measurement kernel in the specified order.

For selection_on_true_then_measurement:

Step 1. Compute p_0 * s over the support.

Step 2. Convolve with K.

Step 3. Renormalise over the support.

For selection_on_measured:

Step 1. Convolve p_0 with K.

Step 2. Multiply by s.

Step 3. Renormalise over the support.

The effective null is returned as a callable density function and as a cumulative distribution function.

Module 6: Continuity Checker
Checks the effective null for continuity.

A density is continuous if it has no jumps larger than the tolerance and no atoms larger than the tolerance.

A density is continuous_with_discontinuities if it has jumps larger than the tolerance but no atoms larger than the tolerance.

A density is atomic if it has atoms larger than the tolerance but is not purely discrete.

A density is discrete if it is supported on a countable set.

The checker reports the continuity status and the list of atoms and discontinuities.

Module 7: Window Mass Calculator
Computes the integral of the effective null over any window w. The integral is computed by numerical integration over the output grid, or by the cumulative distribution function if available.

Module 8: Warning Generator
Generates warnings for the following conditions:

The effective null has atoms, which removes the distribution-free shortcut of Section 3 of the Null-Model Protocol paper.

The effective null has discontinuities.

The effective null is not continuous, which requires full-pipeline simulation (no distribution-free shortcut).

The selection function is not bounded away from zero, which may cause the renormalisation to fail.

The measurement kernel has a bandwidth that is comparable to the gap width, which may blur a real gap.

The base null is a KDE and the bandwidth is comparable to the gap width, which may leak density into the gap (Artifact 1, Section 5.2 heuristic: treat the KDE null as unreliable if h exceeds about g_min/3).

Module 9: Reproducibility Recorder
Records the schema version, builder version, all inputs, and the computation order.

Module 10: Command-Line Interface
Parses arguments. Invokes Modules 1 through 9. Writes the effective null and the continuity status to a file.

Module 11: Library API
Provides the same functionality as the command-line tool, as a library.

Full Computation Specification
6.1 Base Null
The base null p_0 is a density on the support [a, b]. It must integrate to 1 over the support.

For uniform: p_0(x) = 1 / (b - a) on [a, b], 0 elsewhere.

For normal: p_0(x) = (1 / (sigma * sqrt(2 * pi))) * exp(-(x - mu)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)), truncated to [a, b] and renormalised.

For exponential: p_0(x) = lambda * exp(-lambda * x), truncated to [a, b] and renormalised.

For log-normal: p_0(x) = (1 / (x * sigma * sqrt(2 * pi))) * exp(-(log(x) - mu)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)), truncated to [a, b] and renormalised.

For power law: p_0(x) = (alpha - 1) * x^(-alpha) / (a^(1 - alpha) - b^(1 - alpha)), on [a, b].

For gamma: p_0(x) = (x^(k - 1) * exp(-x / theta)) / (theta^k * Gamma(k)), truncated to [a, b] and renormalised.

For beta: p_0(x) = (x^(alpha - 1) * (1 - x)^(beta - 1)) / B(alpha, beta), on [0, 1], or scaled to [a, b].

For mixture: p_0(x) = sum over i of w_i * p_i(x), where the w_i sum to 1 and each p_i is a base null.

For empirical: p_0(x) is a step function over the empirical distribution, or a smoothed version.

For KDE: p_0(x) = (1 / (n * h)) * sum over i of K((x - x_i) / h), where h is the bandwidth.

6.2 Selection Function
The selection function s(x) is the probability that an observation at x enters the sample. It is in [0, 1] and may be zero on some regions.

For analytic: s(x) is a closed-form expression.

For tabulated: s(x) is interpolated from a table.

For stepwise: s(x) is constant on each interval.

6.3 Measurement Kernel
The measurement kernel K(x, y) is the probability density of observing x given a true value y. It integrates to 1 over x for each y.

For Gaussian (homoscedastic): K(x, y) = (1 / (sigma * sqrt(2 * pi))) * exp(-(x - y)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)).

For Gaussian (heteroscedastic): K(x, y) = (1 / (sigma(y) * sqrt(2 * pi))) * exp(-(x - y)^2 / (2 * sigma(y)^2)).

For uniform: K(x, y) = 1 / (2 * delta) if |x - y| <= delta, else 0.

For exponential: K(x, y) = lambda * exp(-lambda * (x - y)) if x >= y, else 0.

For user-supplied: K(x, y) is a callable.

6.4 Effective Null Construction
For selection_on_true_then_measurement:

Step 1. Compute f(x) = p_0(x) * s(x) for all x in the support.

Step 2. Compute g(x) = integral over y of f(y) * K(x, y) dy. This is the convolution of f with K.

Step 3. Compute the normalisation constant Z = integral over x of g(x) dx over the support.

Step 4. The effective null is g(x) / Z for all x in the support.

For selection_on_measured:

Step 1. Compute h(x) = integral over y of p_0(y) * K(x, y) dy. This is the convolution of p_0 with K.

Step 2. Compute g(x) = h(x) * s(x) for all x in the support.

Step 3. Compute the normalisation constant Z = integral over x of g(x) dx over the support.

Step 4. The effective null is g(x) / Z for all x in the support.

6.5 Continuity Check
A density is continuous if for all x in the support, the limit from the left equals the limit from the right, and there are no atoms.

An atom at x is a point mass. In a continuous density, the mass at any single point is zero. If the effective null has a point mass at x, then the density is atomic.

A discontinuity at x is a jump in the density. The jump size is the absolute difference between the limit from the left and the limit from the right.

The continuity checker evaluates the density on the output grid and computes the jumps between adjacent grid points. If any jump exceeds the tolerance, the density is discontinuous. If any grid point has a mass exceeding the tolerance, the density is atomic.

6.6 Window Mass
The window mass over a window w = [w_lo, w_hi] is the integral of the effective null over w. It is computed by numerical integration, or by the cumulative distribution function if available.

6.7 Warnings
Warnings are generated for the conditions listed in Module 8.

6.8 The Distribution-Free Shortcut
If the effective null is continuous, and the observations are independent and identically distributed from the effective null, and the support is fixed (S1), and the statistic Lambda is computed with P_0 taken from the effective null, then the null distribution of Lambda_max is distribution-free (Section 3 of the Null-Model Protocol paper).

If the effective null is not continuous, the distribution-free shortcut does not apply, and full-pipeline simulation must be used.

The effective-null builder reports the continuity status so the analyst knows whether the shortcut applies.

Core Logic as Executable Pseudocode
7.1 Main Flow
function buildEffectiveNull(baseNull, selectionFunction, measurementKernel, order, support, options):

Step 1. validateInputs(baseNull, selectionFunction, measurementKernel, order, support).

Step 2. if order == "selection_on_true_then_measurement":

f = multiply(baseNull, selectionFunction).

g = convolve(f, measurementKernel).

else if order == "selection_on_measured":

h = convolve(baseNull, measurementKernel).

g = multiply(h, selectionFunction).

else: return {error: "unknown order"}.

Step 3. Z = integrate(g, support).

effectiveNull = divide(g, Z).

Step 4. continuityStatus = checkContinuity(effectiveNull, support, options.continuityTolerance).

Step 5. warnings = generateWarnings(baseNull, selectionFunction, measurementKernel, continuityStatus, options).

Step 6. record = buildRecord(baseNull, selectionFunction, measurementKernel, order, support, effectiveNull, continuityStatus, warnings).

Step 7. return {effectiveNull, continuityStatus, warnings, record}.

7.2 Multiply
function multiply(baseNull, selectionFunction):

result = {}.

for x in baseNull.grid:

result[x] = baseNull.value(x) * selectionFunction.value(x).

return result.

7.3 Convolve
function convolve(density, kernel):

result = {}.

for x in density.grid:

integral = 0.

for y in density.grid:

integral = integral + density.value(y) * kernel.value(x, y) * density.gridSpacing.

result[x] = integral.

return result.

7.4 Integrate
function integrate(density, support):

integral = 0.

for x in density.grid:

if x >= support.lo and x <= support.hi:

integral = integral + density.value(x) * density.gridSpacing.

return integral.

7.5 Divide
function divide(density, Z):

result = {}.

for x in density.grid:

result[x] = density.value(x) / Z.

return result.

7.6 Check Continuity
function checkContinuity(density, support, tolerance):

atoms = [].

discontinuities = [].

for x in density.grid:

if density.value(x) > tolerance and isPointMass(density, x):

atoms.push({location: x, mass: density.value(x)}).

for adjacent x1, x2 in density.grid:

jump = abs(density.value(x2) - density.value(x1)).

if jump > tolerance:

discontinuities.push({location: (x1 + x2) / 2, jump: jump}).

if atoms.length > 0 and discontinuities.length > 0: return "atomic".

if atoms.length > 0: return "atomic".

if discontinuities.length > 0: return "continuous_with_discontinuities".

return "continuous".

7.7 Generate Warnings
function generateWarnings(baseNull, selectionFunction, measurementKernel, continuityStatus, options):

warnings = [].

if continuityStatus == "atomic": warnings.push({code: "ATOMIC_EFFECTIVE_NULL", message: "effective null has atoms; distribution-free shortcut does not apply"}).

if continuityStatus == "continuous_with_discontinuities": warnings.push({code: "DISCONTINUOUS_EFFECTIVE_NULL", message: "effective null has discontinuities"}).

if continuityStatus == "discrete": warnings.push({code: "DISCRETE_EFFECTIVE_NULL", message: "effective null is discrete"}).

if selectionFunction != null and minValue(selectionFunction) == 0: warnings.push({code: "SELECTION_ZERO_REGION", message: "selection function is zero on some region; renormalisation may fail"}).

if measurementKernel != null and measurementKernel.bandwidth > options.gap_width / 3: warnings.push({code: "KERNEL_BANDWIDTH_LARGE", message: "measurement kernel bandwidth is comparable to the gap width; may blur a real gap"}).

if baseNull.type == "KDE" and baseNull.bandwidth > options.gap_width / 3: warnings.push({code: "KDE_BANDWIDTH_LARGE", message: "KDE bandwidth exceeds about g_min/3; treat the KDE null as unreliable (Artifact 1, Section 5.2)"}).

return warnings.

7.8 Build Record
function buildRecord(baseNull, selectionFunction, measurementKernel, order, support, effectiveNull, continuityStatus, warnings):

return {schema_version: options.schemaVersion, builder_version: options.builderVersion, base_null: baseNull, selection_function: selectionFunction, measurement_kernel: measurementKernel, order: order, support: support, effective_null_grid: effectiveNull.grid, effective_null_values: effectiveNull.values, continuity_status: continuityStatus, atoms: continuityStatus == "atomic" ? effectiveNull.atoms : [], discontinuities: continuityStatus == "continuous_with_discontinuities" ? effectiveNull.discontinuities : [], warnings: warnings}.

